@@ -216,7 +216,7 @@
         "body": "The story of the bridge began in Takua Pa during the tin mining era, when the Boon Soong tin dredging company was located on the opposite side of the old market. The Takua Pa canal, over 40 metres wide, made it necessary for workers and villagers to row boats back and forth, which was especially difficult during floods. The local database of Prince of Songkla University notes that Mr Chuti Boon Soong, a mining entrepreneur, initially initiated a wooden bridge for walking and cycling. Later, in 1968 (2511 BE), steel from a decommissioned tin dredger was assembled into the Khok Khanoon iron bridge, over 200 metres long, crossing the Takua Pa river. The bridge was not merely an industrial structure, but a shortcut for labour, a route for transporting goods, and a daily connection between the old market and the mining area of a town driven by tin. Verifiable evidence confirms the role of mining and commuting, but contemporary documents linking the bridge directly to maritime trade, Hokkien Chinese people, or the governor system have not been found."
       },
       "before": {
-        "headline": "When the mines went quiet, the bridge became a mining town trace",
+        "headline": "When the mines went quiet, the bridge became a trace of the town's mining past",
         "body": "When the mining business ceased, the bridge did not disappear with the ore dredger, but changed its role from a working structure to a community transit route and evidence of the industrial landscape. The ONEP database states that the old ore dredger in its original condition can still be seen from the bridge area, while contemporary travel sources call this bridge a symbol of Takua Pa's mining past. In later periods, the bridge was told and presented as a tourist attraction and photo spot, and also appeared as a location in the film Wonderful Town (2007) according to location tracking articles. However, this review has not verified the exact year the mine closed, the year of major restorations, direct impacts from the 2004 tsunami, or registration as a historical site by the Fine Arts Department, so these events should not be added as facts."
       },
       "now": {
@@ -279,4 +279,4 @@
       "News from the past 5 years confirming restoration projects, traditional events, or awards specific to the bridge not found."
     ]
   }
-}
\ No newline at end of file
+}
